		<item>
		<title>Anthropic says the key to agent memory is knowing what to forget, not how much to store</title>
		<link>https://the-decoder.com/anthropic-says-the-key-to-agent-memory-is-knowing-what-to-forget-not-how-much-to-store/</link>
					<comments>https://the-decoder.com/anthropic-says-the-key-to-agent-memory-is-knowing-what-to-forget-not-how-much-to-store/#respond</comments>
		
		<dc:creator><![CDATA[Jonathan Kemper]]></dc:creator>
		<pubDate>Mon, 16 Nov 2026 15:25:48 +0000</pubDate>
				<category><![CDATA[AI research]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Artificial Intelligence]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Anthropic]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Claude]]></category>
		<guid isPermaLink="false">https://the-decoder.com/?p=42905</guid>

					<description><![CDATA[<p><img width="1376" height="768" src="https://the-decoder.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/anthropic_claude-2.png" class="attachment-full size-full wp-post-image" alt="" style="height: auto; margin-bottom: 10px;" decoding="async" /></p>
<p>        Anthropic studied how Claude retains knowledge across sessions. Selective notes beat exhaustive archives by a wide margin. In a Pokémon Red test, the newer Claude Fable 5 got four times as far as the older Sonnet 3.7 by keeping just a handful of strategy files instead of more than 400. A "Dreaming" process retroactively corrects faulty memories.</p>
<p>The article <a href="https://the-decoder.com/anthropic-says-the-key-to-agent-memory-is-knowing-what-to-forget-not-how-much-to-store/">Anthropic says the key to agent memory is knowing what to forget, not how much to store</a> appeared first on <a href="https://the-decoder.com">The Decoder</a>.</p>
]]></description>
		
					<wfw:commentRss>https://the-decoder.com/anthropic-says-the-key-to-agent-memory-is-knowing-what-to-forget-not-how-much-to-store/feed/</wfw:commentRss>
